"### Monopoly dynamics \u2014 order-book winner-take-most vs AMM fragmentation\n\n**Derivatives fees are a near-monopoly and HYPE owns it.** Hyperliquid Perps take **45%** of *all* on-chain derivatives fees (30d). The category HHI is **2,864** (monopoly-like \u2014 >2,500 = monopoly territory; a pure monopoly is 10,000). By contrast spot DEXs are **concentrated** (HHI **1,623**): Uniswap ~**31%**, Curve ~**1%**, the rest fragmented.\n\n**Why the asymmetry \u2014 the moat.** An on-chain *order book* is winner-take-most: liquidity begets tighter spreads begets more liquidity, so one venue dominates (like a TradFi exchange \u2014 CME in rates, ICE in energy). An *AMM* (Uniswap/Curve) fragments across pools and forks, so no one venue monopolises. Hyperliquid ported the exchange dynamic on-chain \u2014 that is the structural reason it looks more like **Binance/CME** than like Uniswap.\n\n**vs the CEXs.** Binance & Coinbase are bigger absolute venues, but Hyperliquid already clears **~$2.08B/day** and ~6% of the *total* (CEX+DEX) perps market. The monopoly it holds is the **on-chain** slice \u2014 and unlike a CEX it captures the full stack (matching + settlement + token) and routes ~all of it to holders via buyback.\n\n<sub>Shares = 30-day protocol fees, DefiLlama (`/overview/fees`, live). CEX figures are context (Binance private; Coinbase = public co). HHI = \u03a3(share\u00b2)\u00d710,000. Facts, not forecasts.</sub>"